window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t": "Home", "u": "index.html", "p": "Start here", "x": "Everything AI One place for the whole journey: Python, data science, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI. It is written as a study guide, with the ideas explained plainly and the code you actually need. 17 pages Runnable code examples Searchable glossary Press / to search \ud83d\udc0d Python Type"}, {"t": "How the pieces fit together", "u": "index.html#how-the-pieces-fit-together", "p": "Home", "x": "AI is a stack. Each layer relies on the one below it, so the order of the pages is the order that works best for most people. Layer What it gives you Page Python The tool you write everything in Python Math and data skills Arrays, vectors, statistics, clean data, charts Data Science Classical ML Models that learn from tabular data Machine Learning Deep learning Models for images, sequences and lan"}, {"t": "Python", "u": "index.html#python", "p": "Home", "x": "Types, control flow, functions, classes and threads. The foundation for everything else. \ud83d\udcca"}, {"t": "Data Science", "u": "index.html#data-science", "p": "Home", "x": "NumPy, linear algebra, statistics, hypothesis testing, pandas and plotting. \ud83e\udd16"}, {"t": "Machine Learning", "u": "index.html#machine-learning", "p": "Home", "x": "Regression, classification, clustering, ensembles and recommender systems. \ud83e\udde0"}, {"t": "Deep Learning", "u": "index.html#deep-learning", "p": "Home", "x": "Perceptrons to transformers: optimizers, CNNs, RNNs, autoencoders and PyTorch. \u2728"}, {"t": "Generative AI", "u": "index.html#generative-ai", "p": "Home", "x": "How LLMs behave, prompt engineering, calling model APIs and where AI falls short. \ud83e\udded"}, {"t": "Project Playbook", "u": "index.html#project-playbook", "p": "Home", "x": "A step-by-step checklist for taking a dataset to a trusted model. \ud83d\uddc2\ufe0f"}, {"t": "Case Studies", "u": "index.html#case-studies", "p": "Home", "x": "All 14 course projects, from Walmart sales to autonomous driving, and how AI solves each. \ud83d\udee0\ufe0f"}, {"t": "Tools", "u": "index.html#tools", "p": "Home", "x": "Claude, Grok, Cursor, Antigravity, ChatGPT, OpenClaw and OpenCode: what each is and when to use it. \ud83d\udda5\ufe0f"}, {"t": "Models", "u": "index.html#models", "p": "Home", "x": "NVIDIA enterprise hardware, Mac Ultra, DGX Spark and Station, a 16 GB GPU, model types and sizes. \u2699\ufe0f"}, {"t": "Hardware", "u": "index.html#hardware", "p": "Home", "x": "B200, RTX 5080, DGX Station and Spark, Apple M5 and M6 compared by memory bandwidth, and the best job for each. \ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f"}, {"t": "Creating an LLM", "u": "index.html#creating-an-llm", "p": "Home", "x": "Build a language model from scratch on one GPU: hardware limits, seven stages and a starter config. \ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f"}, {"t": "Finetuning an LLM", "u": "index.html#finetuning-an-llm", "p": "Home", "x": "A step-by-step pipeline for improving a local LLM on one 16 GB GPU, with a QLoRA config and a day-1 checklist. \ud83d\udd01"}, {"t": "Loops", "u": "index.html#loops", "p": "Home", "x": "44 repeatable loops for AI agents, engineering, research and writing. Click one to see how it works. \ud83d\udcdc"}, {"t": "History of AI", "u": "index.html#history-of-ai", "p": "Home", "x": "Eight eras from 1943 to today: two rival philosophies, two winters, and the road to transformers and agents. \ud83e\uddec"}, {"t": "AI Pioneers", "u": "index.html#ai-pioneers", "p": "Home", "x": "Goodfellow, Karpathy and Radford: what they built, their timelines, and a tour of Karpathy's GitHub. Not from the class. \ud83d\udcd6"}, {"t": "Glossary", "u": "index.html#glossary", "p": "Home", "x": "Plain-English definitions of the terms that keep coming up."}, {"t": "Python", "u": "python.html", "p": "The language of AI: syntax, functions, OOP, threads", "x": "Python Python is the default language of AI because it reads almost like English and has a library for nearly everything. This page covers the parts of the language you will use every day. Getting set up You need three things: a Python interpreter, an editor or IDE, and a way to install packages. Fo"}, {"t": "Getting set up", "u": "python.html#getting-set-up", "p": "Python", "x": "You need three things: a Python interpreter, an editor or IDE, and a way to install packages. For data work, Jupyter Notebook is the usual choice because you can run code in small cells and see results immediately. python --version pip install numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn jupyter jupyter notebook Habit worth building Use a virtual environment per project ( python -m venv .venv ) so one pro"}, {"t": "Types, variables and operators", "u": "python.html#types-variables-and-operators", "p": "Python", "x": "Python is dynamically typed: a variable takes the type of the value you assign. The core types are int , float , str , bool , and the collections list , tuple , set and dict . Type Example Mutable? Use it for list [1, 2, 3] Yes Ordered collections that change tuple (1, 2) No Fixed records, dictionary keys set {1, 2, 3} Yes Unique items, fast membership tests dict {\"name\": \"Ada\"} Yes Key-value look"}, {"t": "Decisions and loops", "u": "python.html#decisions-and-loops", "p": "Python", "x": "score = 72 if score >= 90: grade = \"A\" elif score >= 70: grade = \"B\" else: grade = \"C\" for i in range(5): # 0..4 if i == 3: continue # skip this pass if i == 4: break # leave the loop print(i) n = 0 while n < 3: n += 1 else: print(\"loop finished without break\") # runs only if no break break exits the loop, continue skips to the next pass, and the lesser-known else block on a loop runs only when th"}, {"t": "Functions", "u": "python.html#functions", "p": "Python", "x": "A function packages reusable logic. Give it clear inputs and a return value rather than printing inside it. def describe(name, age=30, *tags, **extra): \"\"\"Positional, default, variable-length (*) and keyword (**) arguments.\"\"\" return f\"{name} ({age}) tags={tags} extra={extra}\" describe(\"Ada\", 36, \"ml\", \"math\", city=\"London\") Scope Python looks for a name in this order: L ocal, E nclosing, G lobal,"}, {"t": "Object-oriented programming", "u": "python.html#object-oriented-programming", "p": "Python", "x": "A class is a blueprint and an object is one instance of it. Libraries like scikit-learn and PyTorch are built from classes, so reading them gets much easier once this clicks. class Account: def __init__(self, owner, balance=0): self.owner = owner self._balance = balance # leading underscore: \"treat as private\" def deposit(self, amount): if amount <= 0: raise ValueError(\"amount must be positive\") s"}, {"t": "Threads", "u": "python.html#threads", "p": "Python", "x": "Threads let a program do several things at once, which helps when work spends its time waiting, such as network calls or file reads. Because of the Global Interpreter Lock, threads do not speed up CPU-heavy Python code in the standard build; use multiprocessing for that. (Newer Python versions offer an optional free-threaded build that removes this limit.) import threading, time def work(n): time."}, {"t": "Files and data formats", "u": "python.html#files-and-data-formats", "p": "Python", "x": "import csv, json with open(\"data.csv\", newline=\"\") as f: rows = list(csv.DictReader(f)) with open(\"out.json\", \"w\") as f: json.dump(rows, f, indent=2) JSON and dictionaries JSON is how most web APIs send data, and a JSON object maps almost one-to-one onto a Python dictionary. If you understand dictionaries, you already understand JSON. This comes up again on the Generative AI page."}, {"t": "More from the class: Python details that get asked", "u": "python.html#more-from-the-class-python-details-that-get-asked", "p": "Python", "x": "From the live classes and lessons Details the instructors stressed that the sections above only touch on. Basics Identifiers: cannot start with a digit, are case-sensitive, and cannot be keywords. Notebooks use the .ipynb extension. REPL = read-eval-print loop. A missing indent raises IndentationError . Variables are references. id() shows the object's memory address. y = x shares one object; reas"}, {"t": "Strings", "u": "python.html#strings", "p": "Python", "x": "Strings are immutable sequences. Slice them with s[start:stop:step] , and use f-strings for formatting. Methods like .split() , .strip() , .replace() and .join() cover most cleaning jobs."}, {"t": "Scope", "u": "python.html#scope", "p": "Python", "x": "Python looks for a name in this order: L ocal, E nclosing, G lobal, B uilt-in. Assigning inside a function creates a local variable unless you declare global or nonlocal . In practice, pass values in and return results out."}, {"t": "Generators, lambdas and comprehensions", "u": "python.html#generators-lambdas-and-comprehensions", "p": "Python", "x": "def squares(n): # generator: yields one value at a time for i in range(n): yield i * i evens = [x for x in range(20) if x % 2 == 0] # list comprehension lookup = {w: len(w) for w in [\"ai\", \"data\"]} # dict comprehension by_len = sorted([\"aaa\", \"b\", \"cc\"], key=lambda s: len(s)) Generators matter for l"}, {"t": "Basics", "u": "python.html#basics", "p": "Python", "x": "Identifiers: cannot start with a digit, are case-sensitive, and cannot be keywords. Notebooks use the .ipynb extension. REPL = read-eval-print loop. A missing indent raises IndentationError . Variables are references. id() shows the object's memory address. y = x shares one object; reassigning creat"}, {"t": "Loops and range", "u": "python.html#loops-and-range", "p": "Python", "x": "range(stop) , range(start, stop) and range(start, stop, step) exclude the stop value. A step cannot be 0, and a negative step needs start > stop. range returns a range object; wrap it in list() to see it. The three loop styles: count-controlled, condition-controlled ( while ) and collection-controll"}, {"t": "Functions", "u": "python.html#functions-2", "p": "Python", "x": "Arguments: positional vs keyword. Defaults must come after required parameters. A missing required argument raises TypeError . return : a function with no return gives None , nothing after return runs, and you can return several values (as a tuple). Scope: a local variable can shadow a global one. R"}, {"t": "Object-oriented programming", "u": "python.html#object-oriented-programming-2", "p": "Python", "x": "__init__ is the constructor, called automatically when an object is created; self is the instance. Instance attributes belong to one object; a class attribute is shared by all instances (for example an object counter). Python has automatic garbage collection, so you rarely need a destructor ( __del_"}, {"t": "Threads", "u": "python.html#threads-2", "p": "Python", "x": "You can subclass threading.Thread and override __init__ and run() . A lock has acquire() and release() . Race condition: two threads change shared data at once and the result depends on timing. Locks give mutual exclusion but can cause deadlock if threads wait on each other. Threads are lightweight "}, {"t": "Data Science", "u": "data-science.html", "p": "NumPy, linear algebra, statistics, probability, pandas, visualization", "x": "Data Science Data science is the craft of turning raw data into decisions. It rests on four skills: fast numerical computing, a little linear algebra, statistical thinking, and clean tabular data you can chart. The data science process Frame the question. What decision will this inform, and how will"}, {"t": "The data science process", "u": "data-science.html#the-data-science-process", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Frame the question. What decision will this inform, and how will you know it worked? Collect data. Files, databases, APIs, logs. Clean and prepare. Missing values, duplicates, wrong types, outliers. This usually takes the most time. Explore. Summaries and charts to understand distributions and relationships. Model. Statistics or machine learning to answer the question. Communicate and monitor. Sha"}, {"t": "NumPy", "u": "data-science.html#numpy", "p": "Data Science", "x": "NumPy gives you the ndarray , a fixed-type n-dimensional array that runs operations in compiled code. It is far faster than Python lists and is the base of pandas, scikit-learn and the deep learning libraries. import numpy as np a = np.array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]) a.shape # (2, 3): axis 0 = rows, axis 1 = columns a.sum(axis=0) # column sums -> [5, 7, 9] a.mean(axis=1) # row means -> [2., 5.] a[a "}, {"t": "Linear algebra you actually use", "u": "data-science.html#linear-algebra-you-actually-use", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Idea What it is Why it matters Vector An ordered list of numbers One data point with many features Dot product Sum of element-wise products Similarity, and the core of every neuron Norm The length of a vector Distances, regularization Matrix A grid of numbers A whole dataset, or a transformation Transpose Swap rows and columns Aligning shapes for multiplication Rank Number of linearly independent "}, {"t": "Descriptive statistics", "u": "data-science.html#descriptive-statistics", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Types of data Categorical data is nominal (no order, such as colour) or ordinal (ordered, such as small, medium, large). Numerical data is discrete (counts) or continuous (measurements). The type decides which summaries and charts make sense. Centre and spread Measure Use when Mean Data is roughly symmetric with no big outliers Median Data is skewed or has outliers (income, house prices) Mode Cate"}, {"t": "Probability and distributions", "u": "data-science.html#probability-and-distributions", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Distribution Models Example Bernoulli One yes/no trial One coin flip Binomial Number of successes in n trials Heads in 10 flips Poisson Count of events in a fixed interval Support tickets per hour Uniform All outcomes equally likely A fair die Normal Many natural measurements, bell-shaped Heights, measurement error The probability mass function (discrete) or density function (continuous) says how "}, {"t": "Hypothesis testing", "u": "data-science.html#hypothesis-testing", "p": "Data Science", "x": "You start by assuming nothing interesting is happening (the null hypothesis , H0), then ask whether the data is surprising enough to reject that assumption in favour of the alternative (H1). p-value: the probability of seeing data at least this extreme if H0 were true. A small value (commonly below 0.05) is evidence against H0. It is not the probability that H0 is true. Type I error: rejecting a t"}, {"t": "pandas", "u": "data-science.html#pandas", "p": "Data Science", "x": "pandas gives you the Series (one labelled column) and the DataFrame (a table). It is the main tool for loading, cleaning and reshaping data. import pandas as pd df = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\", parse_dates=[\"date\"]) df.head(); df.info(); df.describe() df[\"revenue\"] = df[\"price\"] * df[\"qty\"] df[df[\"region\"] == \"West\"].sort_values(\"revenue\", ascending=False) df.groupby(\"region\")[\"revenue\"].agg([\"sum\", "}, {"t": "Regular expressions and databases", "u": "data-science.html#regular-expressions-and-databases", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Regular expressions find patterns in text: re.findall(r\"\\d{3}-\\d{4}\", text) . For structured storage, Python's sqlite3 module or pandas.read_sql lets you query a database and get a DataFrame back."}, {"t": "Visualization", "u": "data-science.html#visualization", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Pick the chart for the question, not for the look. Question Chart How is one variable distributed? Histogram, box plot How do two numbers relate? Scatter plot How does something change over time? Line chart How do categories compare? Bar chart How do many variables correlate? Heatmap of the correlation matrix import matplotlib.pyplot as plt import seaborn as sns fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, "}, {"t": "More from the class: statistics and pandas details", "u": "data-science.html#more-from-the-class-statistics-and-pandas-details", "p": "Data Science", "x": "From the lessons Rules, formulas and defaults the instructors taught that the sections above summarise only briefly. Levels of measurement Level Meaning Example Nominal Categories, no order Colour, city Ordinal Ordered, gaps not equal Small / medium / large Interval Equal spacing, no true zero Temperature in Celsius Ratio Equal spacing and a true zero Kelvin, height, price Sets: sample space (all "}, {"t": "Types of data", "u": "data-science.html#types-of-data", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Categorical data is nominal (no order, such as colour) or ordinal (ordered, such as small, medium, large). Numerical data is discrete (counts) or continuous (measurements). The type decides which summaries and charts make sense."}, {"t": "Centre and spread", "u": "data-science.html#centre-and-spread", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Measure Use when Mean Data is roughly symmetric with no big outliers Median Data is skewed or has outliers (income, house prices) Mode Categorical data, or finding the most common value Variance, standard deviation How far values typically sit from the mean IQR A spread measure that ignores extreme "}, {"t": "Cleaning checklist", "u": "data-science.html#cleaning-checklist", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Nulls: df.isna().sum() to find them; dropna() or fillna() to handle them. Choose by understanding why they are missing. Duplicates: df.drop_duplicates() . Types: astype() , pd.to_datetime() , pd.to_numeric(errors=\"coerce\") . Binning: pd.cut and pd.qcut turn numbers into ranges. Scaling: normalize to"}, {"t": "Levels of measurement", "u": "data-science.html#levels-of-measurement", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Level Meaning Example Nominal Categories, no order Colour, city Ordinal Ordered, gaps not equal Small / medium / large Interval Equal spacing, no true zero Temperature in Celsius Ratio Equal spacing and a true zero Kelvin, height, price Sets: sample space (all outcomes), event (a subset), union, int"}, {"t": "Shape of a distribution", "u": "data-science.html#shape-of-a-distribution", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Skewness is 0 for a normal distribution. Negative = longer left tail, positive = longer right tail. Kurtosis is 3 for a normal distribution. Excess kurtosis (kurtosis minus 3) is 0 for normal, positive for heavy tails and outliers, negative for light tails."}, {"t": "Distributions", "u": "data-science.html#distributions", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Binomial: P(X = r) = nCr \u00b7 p r \u00b7 q n-r with q = 1 - p . Poisson: mean = variance = \u03bb, standard deviation = \u221a\u03bb. Used when there are many opportunities and each is unlikely."}, {"t": "Estimation and confidence", "u": "data-science.html#estimation-and-confidence", "p": "Data Science", "x": "A point estimate is one number; an interval estimate is a range. A good estimator is unbiased, has minimum variance (efficient) and is consistent. Confidence level is commonly 95% or 99%. The margin of error is the half-width of the interval; smaller is better. t or z? Use t when n < 30 and the popu"}, {"t": "Chi-square, F and ANOVA", "u": "data-science.html#chi-square-f-and-anova", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Chi-square goodness of fit is a non-parametric (Pearson) test with df = k - 1 . Steps: state hypotheses, choose \u03b1 (usually 0.05), compute df, apply the decision rule. Reject H0 when p < \u03b1 . Shapiro-Wilk and Kolmogorov-Smirnov are other goodness-of-fit tests. F-distribution: the ratio of two chi-squa"}, {"t": "Linear algebra", "u": "data-science.html#linear-algebra", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Vectors are linearly independent when none of them can be written as a combination of the others."}, {"t": "pandas defaults and idioms", "u": "data-science.html#pandas-defaults-and-idioms", "p": "Data Science", "x": "loc selects by label, iloc by integer position. Statistics skip missing values by default ( skipna=True ); std uses ddof=1 ; corr defaults to Pearson. pivot_table defaults to aggfunc=\"mean\" . Window functions: rolling and expanding . Dates: DatetimeIndex , date_range and Timedelta . Method chaining "}, {"t": "Chart design (Tufte)", "u": "data-science.html#chart-design-tufte", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Graphical integrity: do not distort the data. Maximize the data-ink ratio and remove chart junk. Prefer high data density and small multiples (many small charts on the same scale) over one cluttered chart."}, {"t": "Machine Learning", "u": "machine-learning.html", "p": "Regression, classification, clustering, ensembles, recommenders", "x": "Machine Learning Machine learning is software that improves at a task by learning patterns from examples instead of following hand-written rules. This page walks from the core ideas to the algorithms you will reach for most. The big picture Type You give it It learns to Examples Supervised Inputs wi"}, {"t": "The big picture", "u": "machine-learning.html#the-big-picture", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Type You give it It learns to Examples Supervised Inputs with known answers (labels) Predict the label for new inputs Price prediction, spam detection Unsupervised Inputs only Find structure Customer segments, anomaly detection Reinforcement An environment and rewards Choose actions that earn more reward Game playing, robotics Supervised problems split in two. If the target is a number (sales next"}, {"t": "The ML pipeline", "u": "machine-learning.html#the-ml-pipeline", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Define the problem and the metric that counts as success. Prepare data: clean, encode categories, scale, split. Train one or more models on the training set. Evaluate on data the model has not seen. Tune and compare models and settings. Deploy and monitor (MLOps): track performance, retrain when data drifts."}, {"t": "Generalization: overfitting and underfitting", "u": "machine-learning.html#generalization-overfitting-and-underfitting", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "A model should do well on new data, not just the data it studied. Underfitting Overfitting Symptom Poor on training and test data Great on training, poor on test Cause Model too simple or too few features Model memorized noise Fixes More capacity, better features More data, simpler model, regularization, cross-validation, early stopping from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_v"}, {"t": "Regression", "u": "machine-learning.html#regression", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Linear regression Fits a straight line (or plane) y = b0 + b1\u00b7x1 + ... by minimizing squared error. It works best when these assumptions roughly hold: a linear relationship, independent errors, constant error variance, errors that are roughly normal, and little multicollinearity between features. from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error, r2_s"}, {"t": "Classification", "u": "machine-learning.html#classification", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Choosing a metric Metric Answers Prefer when Accuracy What share was right? Classes are balanced Precision Of those flagged positive, how many truly were? False alarms are costly (spam filter) Recall Of all true positives, how many did we catch? Misses are costly (disease screening) F1 Balance of precision and recall Imbalanced classes ROC-AUC How well does it rank positives above negatives? Compa"}, {"t": "Unsupervised learning", "u": "machine-learning.html#unsupervised-learning", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Clustering K-means groups points into k clusters: (1) pick k starting centres, (2) assign each point to its nearest centre, (3) move each centre to the mean of its points, (4) repeat until nothing changes. From the live classes Worked by hand in one dimension: with ages and centres 10, 30 and 60, the point 35 is closest to 30 by Euclidean distance (in one dimension that is just the absolute differ"}, {"t": "Ensemble learning", "u": "machine-learning.html#ensemble-learning", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Several models together usually beat one. Errors that differ between models cancel out. Method How it combines Main benefit Example Bagging Train in parallel on random resamples; average or vote Reduces variance Random forest Boosting Train in sequence, each fixing the last one's mistakes Reduces bias AdaBoost, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost Stacking A final model learns how to weight the others Squee"}, {"t": "Recommender systems", "u": "machine-learning.html#recommender-systems", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Recommenders suggest items a person is likely to want. Content-based: recommend items similar to ones the user liked, using item attributes. Collaborative filtering: use the behaviour of many users. User-based finds similar people and borrows their picks. Item-based finds items often liked together. Model-based: learn hidden factors with matrix factorization (SVD) that explain the rating matrix, t"}, {"t": "Where TensorFlow and PyTorch fit in", "u": "machine-learning.html#where-tensorflow-and-pytorch-fit-in", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "The same ideas extend to neural networks, covered on the Deep Learning page. TensorFlow with Keras and PyTorch both let you build models layer by layer and train them on a GPU."}, {"t": "More from the class: how the instructors did it", "u": "machine-learning.html#more-from-the-class-how-the-instructors-did-it", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "From the live classes and lessons Rules of thumb, formulas and project habits the instructors stressed. Where a number is a rule of thumb, it came from the instructor, not from a standard. Framing and data Churn framing: take features from an earlier date and the exit label from a later date, leaving a gap of about 3 to 6 months so the business has time to act. Same-day data is useless, and every "}, {"t": "Linear regression", "u": "machine-learning.html#linear-regression", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Fits a straight line (or plane) y = b0 + b1\u00b7x1 + ... by minimizing squared error. It works best when these assumptions roughly hold: a linear relationship, independent errors, constant error variance, errors that are roughly normal, and little multicollinearity between features. from sklearn.linear_"}, {"t": "Polynomial, Ridge and LASSO", "u": "machine-learning.html#polynomial-ridge-and-lasso", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Polynomial regression adds powers of features to capture curves. Ridge (L2) penalizes large coefficients, shrinking them smoothly. Good when many features each help a little. LASSO (L1) can shrink coefficients all the way to zero, so it doubles as feature selection."}, {"t": "Logistic regression", "u": "machine-learning.html#logistic-regression", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Despite the name, it is a classifier. It passes a linear score through the sigmoid function to produce a probability between 0 and 1, then applies a threshold. It is a strong, explainable baseline. Imbalanced data If only 1% of cases are positive, a model that always says \"no\" is 99% accurate and us"}, {"t": "Choosing a metric", "u": "machine-learning.html#choosing-a-metric", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Metric Answers Prefer when Accuracy What share was right? Classes are balanced Precision Of those flagged positive, how many truly were? False alarms are costly (spam filter) Recall Of all true positives, how many did we catch? Misses are costly (disease screening) F1 Balance of precision and recall"}, {"t": "Algorithms", "u": "machine-learning.html#algorithms", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Algorithm Idea Strengths Watch out for Naive Bayes Bayes' theorem assuming features are independent Fast, good for text Independence is rarely true k-Nearest Neighbors Vote among the k closest training points Simple, no training Slow on big data; needs scaling Decision tree A flowchart of yes/no spl"}, {"t": "Clustering", "u": "machine-learning.html#clustering", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "K-means groups points into k clusters: (1) pick k starting centres, (2) assign each point to its nearest centre, (3) move each centre to the mean of its points, (4) repeat until nothing changes. From the live classes Worked by hand in one dimension: with ages and centres 10, 30 and 60, the point 35 "}, {"t": "Outlier and anomaly detection", "u": "machine-learning.html#outlier-and-anomaly-detection", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Find points that do not fit: far from neighbours (k-NN distance), isolated by random splits (isolation forest), or low-probability under a model. The PyOD library bundles many detectors."}, {"t": "Dimensionality reduction", "u": "machine-learning.html#dimensionality-reduction", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Method Use it to PCA Compress correlated features into fewer uncorrelated components that keep most of the variance SVD The matrix factorization underneath PCA and recommenders ICA Separate mixed signals into independent sources MCA PCA's counterpart for categorical data from sklearn.decomposition i"}, {"t": "Framing and data", "u": "machine-learning.html#framing-and-data", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Churn framing: take features from an earlier date and the exit label from a later date, leaving a gap of about 3 to 6 months so the business has time to act. Same-day data is useless, and every feature must be available at prediction time. Success criteria up front: projects fix an acceptance thresh"}, {"t": "Evaluating and tuning", "u": "machine-learning.html#evaluating-and-tuning", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Overfit rule of thumb: a train-test gap of about 4 to 5 points is fine; above about 8 to 10 points is overfitting; both scores low (under 50 to 60%) is underfitting. Overfit = low bias, high variance; underfit = high bias. Regression metrics: MAE = mean of |error|, MSE = mean of squared error, RMSE "}, {"t": "Regression in depth", "u": "machine-learning.html#regression-in-depth", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Reading coefficients: the slope (coefficient, beta, weight) is the change in y for a one-unit change in x; the intercept is the constant or base value. Coefficients are not comparable across features on different scales. Assumptions and how to check them: linear and additive relationship; residuals "}, {"t": "Algorithms in depth", "u": "machine-learning.html#algorithms-in-depth", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Decision trees: the root is the feature that gives the lowest weighted impurity (Gini or entropy; weighting stops a tiny branch distorting the result). Continuous features split at a threshold. Algorithms: ID3, C4.5, CART. Key controls: max_depth (often enough alone), min_samples_leaf , max_leaf_nod"}, {"t": "Unsupervised learning in depth", "u": "machine-learning.html#unsupervised-learning-in-depth", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Clustering types: centroid (k-means), density (DBSCAN), distribution, hierarchical. K-medoids uses real data points as centres and resists outliers. Hierarchical clustering can be agglomerative (merge up) or divisive (split down); read a dendrogram by cutting the longest vertical line that crosses n"}, {"t": "Delivering a model", "u": "machine-learning.html#delivering-a-model", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Save the scaler and the model together (pickle or joblib). New data goes through the same fitted scaler ( transform , never fit ) with the same column order as training, and predictions may need an inverse transform back to real units. Test-time rules (Mercedes project): decide zero-variance columns"}, {"t": "Deep Learning", "u": "deep-learning.html", "p": "Neural nets, optimizers, CNNs, RNNs, transformers, PyTorch", "x": "Deep Learning Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers to learn features directly from raw data such as pixels, audio and text. It powers modern vision, speech and language systems. Why deep learning, and when Classical ML needs you to design features by hand. Deep networks learn their ow"}, {"t": "Why deep learning, and when", "u": "deep-learning.html#why-deep-learning-and-when", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Classical ML needs you to design features by hand. Deep networks learn their own features, which is why they dominate images, audio and language. The price is that they need lots of data, lots of compute and careful tuning. Classical ML Deep learning Best for Tabular data, small to medium datasets Images, audio, text, very large datasets Features Engineered by you Learned by the network Compute CP"}, {"t": "The perceptron and how networks learn", "u": "deep-learning.html#the-perceptron-and-how-networks-learn", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "A perceptron is one artificial neuron: multiply each input by a weight, add a bias, and pass the sum through an activation function . z = w1*x1 + w2*x2 + ... + b output = activation(z) Activation Shape Typical use Sigmoid Squashes to 0..1 Binary output probability Tanh Squashes to -1..1 Older recurrent nets ReLU max(0, z) Default for hidden layers Softmax Probabilities that sum to 1 Multi-class ou"}, {"t": "TensorFlow and Keras", "u": "deep-learning.html#tensorflow-and-keras", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "TensorFlow works on tensors (n-dimensional arrays). Keras is its friendly model-building layer. Use the Sequential API for a simple stack of layers and the Functional API for branches, multiple inputs or outputs. TensorFlow Playground is a free browser tool for building intuition on tiny networks. import tensorflow as tf from tensorflow import keras model = keras.Sequential([ keras.layers.Input(sh"}, {"t": "Optimizers", "u": "deep-learning.html#optimizers", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "An optimizer decides how to use the gradient. They build on each other: Optimizer Key idea SGD Step downhill using a small batch of data at a time Momentum Keep a running velocity so steps smooth out and roll through flat spots Adagrad Give rarely-updated parameters bigger steps Adadelta, RMSProp Adapt step sizes using a moving window of recent gradients, fixing Adagrad's ever-shrinking steps Adam"}, {"t": "Making training work", "u": "deep-learning.html#making-training-work", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Batch normalization rescales each layer's inputs, which speeds training and tolerates higher learning rates. Vanishing and exploding gradients: in deep nets gradients can shrink to nothing or blow up. ReLU, careful weight initialization, normalization, residual connections and gradient clipping help. Dropout randomly switches off neurons during training so the network cannot lean on any one of the"}, {"t": "Convolutional neural networks (CNNs)", "u": "deep-learning.html#convolutional-neural-networks-cnns", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "CNNs are built for images. Instead of connecting every pixel to every neuron, small filters slide across the image and detect local patterns such as edges, then textures, then object parts in later layers. Filters are shared across the image, which cuts parameters dramatically. Layer Does Convolution Applies filters to produce feature maps Pooling (max or average) Shrinks the maps and adds toleran"}, {"t": "Transfer learning", "u": "deep-learning.html#transfer-learning", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Instead of training from scratch, start from a model pretrained on a huge dataset (for example ImageNet) and adapt it. Freeze the early layers, which already know edges and shapes, and train a new final layer on your small dataset. Benefits: far less data and compute, and usually better accuracy. Pick a pretrained model that matches your task and image size, and check its licence. base = keras.app"}, {"t": "Object detection and edge deployment", "u": "deep-learning.html#object-detection-and-edge-deployment", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Classification says what is in an image; object detection also says where, by predicting bounding boxes. YOLO (\"you only look once\") does this in a single pass, which makes it fast enough for video. Preparing the dataset means labelling boxes for each object. For phones and embedded devices, TensorFlow Lite (now called LiteRT) converts a trained model into a smaller, faster format."}, {"t": "Sequence models: RNNs", "u": "deep-learning.html#sequence-models-rnns", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Some data is ordered: text, speech, sensor readings. A recurrent neural network reads one step at a time and carries a hidden state forward as memory. Training uses backpropagation through time. Plain RNNs forget over long spans because of vanishing gradients, which is why LSTM and GRU cells with gates were invented. Hybrid models combine a CNN to read each frame with an RNN to read the sequence, "}, {"t": "Transformers and BERT", "u": "deep-learning.html#transformers-and-bert", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Transformers replaced recurrence with self-attention : every token looks at every other token and decides how much to weigh it. That captures long-range context and trains in parallel on GPUs. Architecture: an encoder stack, a decoder stack, positional encoding (so order is known), multi-head attention and feed-forward layers. BERT uses only the encoder and reads text in both directions. It is pre"}, {"t": "Autoencoders", "u": "deep-learning.html#autoencoders", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "An autoencoder squeezes its input through a narrow bottleneck (the encoder) then rebuilds it (the decoder), trained to make the output match the input. The bottleneck is forced to hold the essence of the data. Uses: compression, denoising, anomaly detection (bad reconstruction means unusual input) and pretraining."}, {"t": "PyTorch", "u": "deep-learning.html#pytorch", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "PyTorch builds the computation graph as your code runs, which makes debugging feel like normal Python. You write the model as a class and the training loop yourself. import torch, torch.nn as nn class Net(nn.Module): def __init__(self): super().__init__() self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(20, 64), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(64, 1)) def forward(self, x): return self.net(x) model, loss_fn = Net(), nn.BCE"}, {"t": "More from the class: details and specifics", "u": "deep-learning.html#more-from-the-class-details-and-specifics", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "From the lessons Parameters, rules and API points the instructors covered that the sections above summarise briefly. Tensors and Keras A tensor has a rank (number of dimensions). Know broadcasting, reshape , element-wise vs matrix multiplication, argmax , and ragged and string tensors. The Functional API needs an explicit Input layer. The class labs used sparse_categorical_crossentropy (integer la"}, {"t": "Training in four repeating steps", "u": "deep-learning.html#training-in-four-repeating-steps", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Forward propagation: push inputs through the layers to get a prediction. Loss: measure how wrong it was. Backpropagation: use the chain rule to work out how much each weight contributed to the error (its gradient). Update: nudge each weight against its gradient, scaled by the learning rate . Repeat "}, {"t": "Loss functions", "u": "deep-learning.html#loss-functions", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Task Loss Regression Mean squared error, mean absolute error Binary classification Binary cross-entropy Multi-class classification Categorical cross-entropy"}, {"t": "Tensors and Keras", "u": "deep-learning.html#tensors-and-keras", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "A tensor has a rank (number of dimensions). Know broadcasting, reshape , element-wise vs matrix multiplication, argmax , and ragged and string tensors. The Functional API needs an explicit Input layer. The class labs used sparse_categorical_crossentropy (integer labels) rather than one-hot categoric"}, {"t": "Optimizers and training", "u": "deep-learning.html#optimizers-and-training", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "SGD learning rate is often 0.01; momentum typically 0.9; RMSProp rho about 0.9 to 0.99. Adam combines momentum and RMSProp with bias correction. Adagrad suits sparse data; the original Adadelta needs no hand-set learning rate. Dropout is active only during training and is not applied to the output l"}, {"t": "CNNs", "u": "deep-learning.html#cnns", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "An image is height \u00d7 width \u00d7 channels (3 for colour) with pixel values 0 to 255; divide by 255 to scale to 0 to 1. Stride is how many pixels the filter moves each step (1 or 2). Padding adds zeros around the image: valid = none (the output shrinks), same = enough to keep the output size. Pooling: ma"}, {"t": "Transfer learning, detection and edge", "u": "deep-learning.html#transfer-learning-detection-and-edge", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Transfer can be positive (helps) or negative (hurts, when the tasks differ too much). Choose a pretrained model by size, file format ( .keras , or the older .h5 , for Keras and TensorFlow; .pt for PyTorch), input and output format, and reported accuracy (for example AP). YOLO is regression-based (on"}, {"t": "Sequences, Transformers, autoencoders", "u": "deep-learning.html#sequences-transformers-autoencoders", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "RNN shapes: one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-one and many-to-many (encoder-decoder). Pros: any input length, shared weights. Cons: slow, exploding or vanishing gradients. Tasks: sentiment, part-of-speech tagging, named-entity recognition. A hybrid CNN + RNN uses the CNN for spatial features and the "}, {"t": "PyTorch", "u": "deep-learning.html#pytorch-2", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "autograd computes gradients, torch.nn holds layers and torch.optim holds optimizers. Dataset and DataLoader feed batches; ToTensor converts images and scales them to 0 to 1; a Lambda transform can one-hot encode labels; DataParallel uses several GPUs."}, {"t": "Generative AI", "u": "generative-ai.html", "p": "LLMs, prompt engineering, APIs, limits and cost", "x": "Generative AI Generative AI creates new content (text, images, code, audio) rather than only labelling or predicting. This page covers how the main model families work, how to steer them with prompts, how to call them from code, and where the real limits are. What makes it \"generative\" A discriminat"}, {"t": "What makes it \"generative\"", "u": "generative-ai.html#what-makes-it-generative", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "A discriminative model answers \"which category is this?\". A generative model learns the shape of the data itself well enough to produce new samples that look like it. The ideas build directly on deep learning . Model family How it generates Typical output Transformer-based (LLMs) Predicts the next token repeatedly, using attention over the whole context Text, code, summaries, translation Diffusion"}, {"t": "How large language models work", "u": "generative-ai.html#how-large-language-models-work", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Tokens: text is split into small pieces (words or parts of words). Models read and write tokens, and usage is usually billed per token. Pretraining: the model reads huge amounts of text and learns to predict the next token. This is where its broad knowledge and fluency come from. Fine-tuning and instruction tuning: further training on examples of good behaviour, so the model follows instructions, "}, {"t": "Prompt engineering", "u": "generative-ai.html#prompt-engineering", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "The prompt is your main control over the output. Good prompting is clear writing, not magic words. Zero-shot, few-shot and beyond Technique What you do Use it when Zero-shot Give an instruction with no examples The task is simple and common Few-shot Include a few worked examples inside the prompt so the model copies the pattern and style You need a specific format, tone or label set Role / persona"}, {"t": "Calling a model from Python", "u": "generative-ai.html#calling-a-model-from-python", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Hosted models are reached through an API . You authenticate with an API key , send a list of messages as JSON, and read the reply. The same pattern works across providers (OpenAI, Groq, Anthropic, and others); only the client and model name change. import os, json from groq import Groq # pip install groq client = Groq(api_key=os.environ[\"GROQ_API_KEY\"]) # never hard-code keys prompt = \"\"\"Extract t"}, {"t": "Giving a model your own knowledge: RAG and the LLM Wiki", "u": "generative-ai.html#giving-a-model-your-own-knowledge-rag-and-the-llm-wiki", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "There are two ways to hand a model your own documents without retraining it. The classic one is RAG . A newer one, published by Andrej Karpathy as the LLM Wiki pattern, has the same goal but changes where the work happens. Classic RAG: retrieve at question time Retrieval-augmented generation keeps the base model unchanged and hands it your data when a question arrives. Split your documents into ch"}, {"t": "Agentic AI", "u": "generative-ai.html#agentic-ai", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "A standard model answers a prompt. An agent goes further: it plans, picks tools (search, code, email, databases), carries out steps and checks the result, with limited human intervention. An onboarding agent, for example, could read a request, create accounts, send emails and log what it did. Self-driving cars are a physical-world cousin. The more freedom an agent has, the more it needs permission"}, {"t": "Responsible AI: bias, privacy and security", "u": "generative-ai.html#responsible-ai-bias-privacy-and-security", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Bias. Models reflect their training data. If the data under-represents or stereotypes a group, outputs will too. Mitigate with diverse, representative data, testing across groups, human review, and reporting problems to the model provider. RAG over vetted data can also help steer outputs. Privacy. Do not paste confidential or personal data into tools you do not control. Privacy-preserving approach"}, {"t": "Will AI replace jobs?", "u": "generative-ai.html#will-ai-replace-jobs", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "The realistic view from the class discussion: generative AI changes how work is done more than it removes whole roles. It speeds up drafting, coding and analysis, so a person can act as a reviewer who validates the output rather than writing everything from scratch. Reasons it does not simply replace people: Cost. Running large models at scale is expensive, so automating everything is often unecon"}, {"t": "A quick checklist before you ship an AI feature", "u": "generative-ai.html#a-quick-checklist-before-you-ship-an-ai-feature", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Is the prompt specific, with a defined output format? Are outputs validated before anything acts on them? Is a person in the loop for high-stakes decisions? Are keys, personal data and secrets kept out of prompts and code? Have you tested for bias and for failure cases? Do you know the cost per request at your expected volume?"}, {"t": "More from the class: governance, tools and prompting tips", "u": "generative-ai.html#more-from-the-class-governance-tools-and-prompting-tips", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "From the live classes Points from the GenAI Literacy sessions that go beyond the sections above. Regulation and governance EU AI Act: classifies AI systems by risk. GDPR: limits how personal data may be stored and used. US executive orders give guidance on responsible AI use. Governance components: risk assessment, compliance monitoring, a human in the loop, and accountability shared among develop"}, {"t": "Zero-shot, few-shot and beyond", "u": "generative-ai.html#zero-shot-few-shot-and-beyond", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Technique What you do Use it when Zero-shot Give an instruction with no examples The task is simple and common Few-shot Include a few worked examples inside the prompt so the model copies the pattern and style You need a specific format, tone or label set Role / persona Say who the model should act "}, {"t": "Best practices", "u": "generative-ai.html#best-practices", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Be specific. State the task, audience, length and what to leave out. Give context. Paste the relevant text or facts instead of assuming the model knows them. Specify the format. Bullet list, table, JSON with named keys, word limit. Set the tone and the role. Show examples when wording alone is ambig"}, {"t": "Weak versus strong", "u": "generative-ai.html#weak-versus-strong", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Weak Strong \"Tell me about this customer issue.\" \"Extract issue summary, likely cause, priority and recommended action from the text below. Return valid JSON with exactly those four keys, plus a two-column markdown table of the same fields. Use a neutral, professional tone.\" The strong version state"}, {"t": "Classic RAG: retrieve at question time", "u": "generative-ai.html#classic-rag-retrieve-at-question-time", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Retrieval-augmented generation keeps the base model unchanged and hands it your data when a question arrives. Split your documents into chunks, turn each into an embedding (a vector of numbers capturing meaning), store them in a vector index, and at question time find the closest chunks and put them"}, {"t": "The LLM Wiki: compile once, keep it current", "u": "generative-ai.html#the-llm-wiki-compile-once-keep-it-current", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "In April 2026 Karpathy published an \"idea file\" (a gist, not code) describing a different pattern: instead of retrieving raw chunks, an LLM builds and maintains a wiki , a set of interlinked Markdown pages, from your sources. When a new source arrives, the LLM reads it, writes a summary, updates the"}, {"t": "How it actually works", "u": "generative-ai.html#how-it-actually-works", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "You add a source to the raw collection and ask the LLM to process it. The LLM reads it and discusses the key takeaways with you. It writes a summary page in the wiki, then updates index.md . It updates the related pages (people, concepts, comparisons), adding links and flagging where the new source "}, {"t": "Three operations", "u": "generative-ai.html#three-operations", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Ingest: read a new source, discuss the takeaways, write its summary page, update the index and related pages, and add an entry to the log. Ingesting one source at a time keeps you in the loop. Query: the LLM reads the index, opens the relevant pages and answers with citations. The answer can be a pa"}, {"t": "Two special files", "u": "generative-ai.html#two-special-files", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "index.md : a catalog of every page with a link and a one-line summary, organized by category. The LLM reads it first when answering. The gist says this works at moderate scale (around 100 sources and a few hundred pages) without embedding infrastructure. log.md : an append-only record of ingests, qu"}, {"t": "RAG, LLM Wiki and fine-tuning compared", "u": "generative-ai.html#rag-llm-wiki-and-fine-tuning-compared", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Classic RAG LLM Wiki Fine-tuning Changes the model? No No Yes When the work happens At question time At ingest time, then reused At training time What is stored Raw chunks and embeddings Readable, linked Markdown pages Changed weights Knowledge accumulates? No Yes: cross-references and syntheses alr"}, {"t": "Regulation and governance", "u": "generative-ai.html#regulation-and-governance", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "EU AI Act: classifies AI systems by risk. GDPR: limits how personal data may be stored and used. US executive orders give guidance on responsible AI use. Governance components: risk assessment, compliance monitoring, a human in the loop, and accountability shared among developers, organizations and "}, {"t": "Security, bias and privacy", "u": "generative-ai.html#security-bias-and-privacy", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Threats named in class: data poisoning, model inversion, deepfakes and misinformation, and prompt injection. Bias mitigation: fairness audits and diverse training data. If the bias comes from the base model you cannot remove it, so report it to the provider; supplying your own data through RAG can c"}, {"t": "Physical AI and model families", "u": "generative-ai.html#physical-ai-and-model-families", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Physical AI acts in the real world through robots, drones, exoskeletons and self-driving cars. Self-driving cars combine physical and agentic AI. Model names mentioned: BERT (bidirectional, for understanding text), GPT (generates text), T5 (text-to-text), and image tools such as Art Breeder, DeepArt"}, {"t": "Tools named in class", "u": "generative-ai.html#tools-named-in-class", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Tool What it is Hugging Face Open-source model hub Groq A host that serves open models fast. Not the same as Grok, xAI's model ElevenLabs AI voice generation HeyGen AI video avatars Unity ML-Agents, NVIDIA Omniverse Simulation for training physical and game agents GitHub Copilot, Cursor AI coding as"}, {"t": "Prompting and API tips", "u": "generative-ai.html#prompting-and-api-tips", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Build reusable prompt templates and train your team to prompt well. Iterate after a first detailed prompt. For resumes and profiles, add \u201cuse only the information given; do not invent anything\u201d because these prompts hallucinate. Cross-check translations with another tool. Ask the model to check itse"}, {"t": "Project Playbook", "u": "playbook.html", "p": "A practical checklist from the live classes", "x": "Project Playbook A practical, end-to-end checklist for taking a dataset to a model you can trust. It pulls together the habits from the lessons and the live classes into one workflow. 1. Frame the problem Write the question in one sentence and say what decision it supports. Classify it: regression, "}, {"t": "1. Frame the problem", "u": "playbook.html#1-frame-the-problem", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Write the question in one sentence and say what decision it supports. Classify it: regression, classification, clustering, recommendation, or generation. Pick the metric that matches the cost of mistakes (see the metrics table ). Decide the baseline to beat: a simple rule, the average, or the current process."}, {"t": "2. Inspect the data", "u": "playbook.html#2-inspect-the-data", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "df.shape; df.dtypes; df.head() df.isna().mean().sort_values(ascending=False).head(10) # worst missing columns df.describe(include=\"all\").T df.duplicated().sum() Look at the target's distribution first. Check class balance before anything else."}, {"t": "3. Clean and prepare", "u": "playbook.html#3-clean-and-prepare", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Remove columns with no information. Zero-variance columns (a single unique value) cannot help any model. Classify columns automatically by unique-value count: binary, low-cardinality categorical, high-cardinality, numeric. Handle missing values deliberately: drop, fill with median or mode, or add an \"is missing\" flag. Encode categories (one-hot for few levels, target or ordinal encoding for many)."}, {"t": "4. Build a baseline, then compare", "u": "playbook.html#4-build-a-baseline-then-compare", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Start with something simple: logistic or linear regression. It is fast and sets the bar. Try a tree-based ensemble (random forest, gradient boosting). They are strong defaults on tabular data. Compare models on the same split or cross-validation folds, using the metric from step 1. from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression from sklearn.model_selection import cross_validate models = {\"log"}, {"t": "5. Diagnose", "u": "playbook.html#5-diagnose", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "You see Likely cause Try Train and validation both poor Underfitting Richer features, more flexible model Train great, validation poor Overfitting More data, regularization, simpler model, early stopping Great accuracy, bad recall on the rare class Imbalance Better metric, class weights, SMOTE on train only Test score far above anything realistic Data leakage Check that no feature reveals the answ"}, {"t": "6. Tune and evaluate once", "u": "playbook.html#6-tune-and-evaluate-once", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Tune hyperparameters with cross-validation on the training data. Touch the test set once, at the very end, to get an honest final number. Report the metric together with a confusion matrix and a sentence on where the model fails."}, {"t": "7. Explain it", "u": "playbook.html#7-explain-it", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Show feature importance or SHAP values. Give one or two concrete example predictions, right and wrong. State the limits: what data it was trained on, and where it should not be trusted."}, {"t": "8. Ship and monitor", "u": "playbook.html#8-ship-and-monitor", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Save the whole pipeline ( joblib.dump(pipe, \"model.joblib\") ), not just the model, so preprocessing travels with it. Log inputs and predictions. Watch for data drift (inputs change) and concept drift (the relationship changes). Set a retraining trigger and keep the previous version for rollback."}, {"t": "Study plan", "u": "playbook.html#study-plan", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Week Focus Proof you got it 1 Python : functions, classes, files Write a script that cleans a CSV 2 Data science : NumPy, pandas, statistics Explore a dataset and run a t-test 3-4 Machine learning : supervised models and metrics Beat a baseline with cross-validation 5 Clustering and dimensionality reduction Segment customers and explain the groups 6-7 Deep learning : networks, CNNs, transfer learn"}, {"t": "Program and certification notes", "u": "playbook.html#program-and-certification-notes", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "From the induction session (March 2026) As described by the program lead at the start of the program. Check the learning portal for the current rules. Structure: seven parts: Python, Data Science, Machine Learning, Deep Learning, Azure AI (preparing for the Azure AI Fundamentals certification. Microsoft retired the AI-900 exam on 30 June 2026 and replaced it with AI-901; the certification itself c"}, {"t": "Case Studies", "u": "case-studies.html", "p": "All 14 course projects and how AI solves each one", "x": "Case Studies The 14 projects in the Microsoft AI Engineer program, and for each one, how AI actually solves it: the problem type, the data, the method, and the trap to avoid. How these were written The project titles come from the course's Assessments tab. The LMS briefs were not read, so each write"}, {"t": "Python For AI", "u": "case-studies.html#python-for-ai", "p": "Case Studies", "x": "Analyzing Customer Orders Using Python Goal: turn a table of orders into answers: revenue by product, best customers, busy periods. How AI does it: this one is mostly not machine learning. It is the foundation AI sits on: load the data with pandas , clean it, group and aggregate, then chart it. The \"AI\" part is optional: a language model can draft the pandas code from a plain-English question, and"}, {"t": "Applied Data Science with Python", "u": "case-studies.html#applied-data-science-with-python", "p": "Case Studies", "x": "Retail Analysis with Walmart Data Goal: understand and predict store sales, and see how holidays, temperature, fuel price, CPI and unemployment affect them. (This matches the well-known Walmart weekly-sales dataset; confirm against your brief.) How AI does it: frame it as regression on weekly sales. Steps: parse dates and add week, month and holiday features; explore which stores and weeks are str"}, {"t": "Machine Learning using Python", "u": "case-studies.html#machine-learning-using-python", "p": "Case Studies", "x": "Mercedes-Benz Greener Manufacturing Goal: predict how long a car takes on the test bench from hundreds of anonymous features, so testing time (and emissions) can be cut. (Based on the public Kaggle competition of the same name.) How AI does it: a regression problem with many binary columns and few rows. Remove zero-variance columns (no information), check for duplicates, encode the few categorical"}, {"t": "Deep Learning Specialization", "u": "case-studies.html#deep-learning-specialization", "p": "Case Studies", "x": "Lending Club Loan Data Analysis Goal: predict whether a borrower will repay a loan. How AI does it: tabular classification with a neural network. Clean and encode the loan features, scale the numeric ones, handle the heavy class imbalance, then train a small feed-forward network (dense layers, ReLU, dropout, sigmoid output) with early stopping, as in the Deep Learning Keras example. Compare it hon"}, {"t": "Microsoft AI Engineer Capstone", "u": "case-studies.html#microsoft-ai-engineer-capstone", "p": "Case Studies", "x": "Preserving Heritage: Enhancing Tourism with AI Goal: the title suggests using AI to protect cultural sites and improve the visitor experience. The exact deliverable is not known from the title, so this entry lists the usual building blocks. How AI could do it: Recognize monuments from photos with a pretrained image model fine-tuned on landmark images. Answer visitor questions with a language model"}, {"t": "The pattern across all 14", "u": "case-studies.html#the-pattern-across-all-14", "p": "Case Studies", "x": "If the target is... It is... Reach for A number (sales, test time) Regression / forecasting Linear regression, gradient boosting, time-series models A yes/no (leaves, defaults, responds) Classification Logistic regression, random forest, small neural net Nothing (find groups) Clustering k-means, hierarchical, PCA first Images Computer vision Pretrained CNN, YOLO Text or code Generative AI LLM with"}, {"t": "Tools", "u": "tools.html", "p": "Claude, Grok, Cursor, Antigravity, ChatGPT, OpenClaw and OpenCode", "x": "Tools Seven AI tools you will run into: Claude, Grok, Cursor, Antigravity, ChatGPT, OpenClaw and OpenCode. What each one is, where you can use it, and when to pick it. Not from the class This page is a reference added on top of the course content. These products change every few months (names, plans"}, {"t": "At a glance", "u": "tools.html#at-a-glance", "p": "Tools", "x": "Two words matter here. A web app is a chat you open in a browser. A CLI (command-line interface) is a program you run in a terminal, where it can read your files, run commands and edit code on your computer. Many tools now offer both. Tool Made by Web app CLI Best known for Claude Anthropic Yes Yes (Claude Code) Writing, reasoning and coding agents Grok xAI Yes Yes (Grok Build) Chat tied to X, plu"}, {"t": "How to choose", "u": "tools.html#how-to-choose", "p": "Tools", "x": "If you want to... Start with Ask questions, draft and learn ChatGPT or Claude (web app) Let an agent change code in a project, from the terminal Claude Code, Grok Build, Antigravity CLI or OpenCode Work inside an editor with visual diffs Cursor (or Antigravity's IDE) Run several coding agents at once Antigravity or Cursor Avoid vendor lock-in OpenCode Message an assistant from your phone that acts"}, {"t": "Sources", "u": "tools.html#sources", "p": "Tools", "x": "Product details come from secondary coverage found in November 2026, not from the makers' own documentation, so verify before you rely on them. xAI releases early beta of Grok Build (RuntimeWire) superagent-ai/grok-cli (community project) Antigravity: desktop app, CLI and SDK (The Next Web) What is OpenClaw? (DigitalOcean) OpenCode overview (Tecmint) Cursor (code editor) (Wikipedia)"}, {"t": "Models", "u": "models.html", "p": "Enterprise NVIDIA, big local machines, a 16 GB GPU, model types and BenchLM", "x": "Models Where AI models actually run. The hardware NVIDIA sells to enterprises for cloud models, the desktop machines that run big models locally (Mac Studio, DGX Spark, DGX Station), what a single 16 GB RTX 5080 can do, the main kinds of models, and how to compare them. Not from the class This page "}, {"t": "The one rule: memory decides everything", "u": "models.html#the-one-rule-memory-decides-everything", "p": "Models", "x": "A model's weights must sit in fast memory while it runs. Rough sizing: file size in GB is about parameters (in billions) \u00d7 bytes per weight . At 16-bit that is 2 bytes, at 8-bit 1 byte, at 4-bit about 0.5 byte. Add room for the KV cache (the model's working memory of the conversation), which grows with context length. Two numbers matter on any machine: capacity (can the model fit at all) and bandw"}, {"t": "NVIDIA in the enterprise: how cloud models run", "u": "models.html#nvidia-in-the-enterprise-how-cloud-models-run", "p": "Models", "x": "Cloud models such as Claude, GPT and Gemini are too large for any one chip, so providers run them across racks of data-center accelerators. NVIDIA sells most of that hardware. Layer What it is Role Data-center GPUs Hopper (H100, H200) and Blackwell (B200, B300) with HBM, very fast stacked memory Train and serve large models. Memory per GPU is roughly 80 to 288 GB depending on generation. HGX / DGX"}, {"t": "Cloud models versus local models", "u": "models.html#cloud-models-versus-local-models", "p": "Models", "x": "Cloud models Local models Where they run Provider's data center Your own computer or server Typical size Frontier models, sizes usually undisclosed, believed to be hundreds of billions to trillions of parameters Roughly 1B to 235B parameters, usually 4-bit compressed Quality Best available, especially for hard reasoning and long agentic work Good and improving fast; the gap is smaller for focused "}, {"t": "Parameter size: what the number means", "u": "models.html#parameter-size-what-the-number-means", "p": "Models", "x": "A parameter is one learned number in the network. More parameters usually means more knowledge and better reasoning, at the cost of memory and speed. Size Examples of what it is good for Memory at 4-bit (approx.) 1B to 4B Phones, autocomplete, classification, simple extraction 1 to 3 GB 7B to 14B Everyday chat, summaries, light coding on a laptop or mid-range GPU 5 to 9 GB 20B to 35B Solid coding "}, {"t": "A desktop RTX 5080 with 16 GB", "u": "models.html#a-desktop-rtx-5080-with-16-gb", "p": "Models", "x": "For how this card compares with the B200, DGX Station and Spark, and Apple's M5 and M6 chips, see the Hardware page. A single gaming card is the most common local setup. The card has 16 GB of fast video memory (VRAM); everything beyond that must spill into system RAM, which is slower. Three models stand out for it: Model Size and type Context How it fits Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (run with Strata ) 125B "}, {"t": "Kinds of models and what they are for", "u": "models.html#kinds-of-models-and-what-they-are-for", "p": "Models", "x": "There is no single best model. Pick by the job. This list starts with the large MoE \"next flash\" style of model and works toward the specialized ones. Type Examples of the kind Use it for Large MoE generalists Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (125B, about 6B active) Near-frontier chat, coding and agent work on a big-memory desktop, with fast generation for the quality. Frontier cloud models Claude, GPT, Gemini,"}, {"t": "Comparing models: BenchLM", "u": "models.html#comparing-models-benchlm", "p": "Models", "x": "BenchLM is a leaderboard site that compares language models on quality, cost and context window. Its own pages report between about 400 tracked models and about 1,000 model profiles (the count changes as it updates), with scores built from published benchmark results rather than private tests. It offers quick picks such as best open-weight model, best value, fastest, and largest useful context, pl"}, {"t": "Sources", "u": "models.html#sources", "p": "Models", "x": "Hardware and model facts come from vendor docs and secondary coverage found in November 2026. BenchLM Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (Unsloth docs) What is Strata? (AIIDE list) DGX Spark and DGX Station (Wccftech) DGX Spark vs DGX Station GB300 (pi3g)"}, {"t": "Dense versus mixture of experts (MoE)", "u": "models.html#dense-versus-mixture-of-experts-moe", "p": "Models", "x": "A dense model uses all its parameters for every token. A mixture-of-experts model holds many specialist sub-networks and activates only a few per token. A \"125B\" MoE model that activates about 6B parameters per token needs memory for all 125B but reads only about 6B per token, so it generates much f"}, {"t": "Quantization", "u": "models.html#quantization", "p": "Models", "x": "Quantization stores each weight with fewer bits. A full-precision weight takes 2 to 4 bytes; a 4-bit weight takes half a byte. The file shrinks, it fits in less memory, and generation gets faster because fewer bytes are read per token (see the bandwidth rule on the Hardware page). The cost is some l"}, {"t": "Precision levels", "u": "models.html#precision-levels", "p": "Models", "x": "Format Bits Quality Used for FP32 32 Reference Training internals; rarely used to run models FP16 / BF16 16 Full quality (BF16 has a wider number range, which makes training steadier) The normal released form of a model; cloud serving FP8 / INT8 8 Close to lossless Data-center serving (H100 and B200"}, {"t": "The two ways to get a quantized model", "u": "models.html#the-two-ways-to-get-a-quantized-model", "p": "Models", "x": "Post-training quantization (PTQ): take a finished model and compress its weights afterward. Quick and cheap, and almost every file you download from the community is made this way. Quality drops more as the bit count falls. Quantization-aware training (QAT): the model is trained or fine-tuned while "}, {"t": "Common file and method types", "u": "models.html#common-file-and-method-types", "p": "Models", "x": "Type What it is Runs in GGUF A single-file format from llama.cpp that holds a quantized model. Names such as Q8_0 , Q6_K , Q5_K_M , Q4_K_M , Q3_K_M and Q2_K give the approximate bit count. K-quants (the _K ones) mix precisions across layers, and the suffix S , M or L means small, medium or large mix"}, {"t": "How to choose", "u": "models.html#how-to-choose", "p": "Models", "x": "Use the highest precision that fits in your fast memory with room left for the context (KV cache). If 8-bit fits, take it; if not, 4-bit. A larger model at 4-bit usually beats a smaller model at 8-bit, so it is often better to quantize a bigger model than to run a small one at full quality. Go below"}, {"t": "Context length", "u": "models.html#context-length", "p": "Models", "x": "The context window is how much text the model can consider at once (128K tokens is roughly a 300-page book). Longer context costs memory for the KV cache and slows prompt processing, so a model that \"supports\" 256K may not fit 256K on your hardware."}, {"t": "Which software runs which model", "u": "models.html#which-software-runs-which-model", "p": "Models", "x": "Gemma 4 12B QAT and gpt-oss-20b are small enough to fit in the 16 GB card, so any of the mainstream engines can run them: Ollama , LM Studio or vLLM . Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (125B) does not fit, and it needs a new architecture that mainline llama.cpp did not support at the time of writing, so it runs th"}, {"t": "Hardware", "u": "hardware.html", "p": "Memory bandwidth and the best use of B200, RTX 5080, DGX, Apple M5 and M6", "x": "Hardware How fast each kind of AI hardware really is, measured by memory bandwidth, and the best job each one can do: NVIDIA B200, RTX 5080, DGX Station, DGX Spark, Apple M5 Ultra, M5 Max and M6. Not from the class This page is a reference added on top of the course content. Specs are a snapshot fro"}, {"t": "The parts of a computer, and what each does for AI", "u": "hardware.html#the-parts-of-a-computer-and-what-each-does-for-ai", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Four components decide how a machine runs a model. Knowing each one explains the bandwidth numbers further down. Part What it does Role in AI Typical speed CPU (central processing unit) A few to a few dozen powerful cores that run the operating system, programs and logic one step after another, very flexibly Runs your code, loads data, schedules the work, tokenizes text. Can run small models on it"}, {"t": "RAM versus VRAM, and why unified memory helps", "u": "hardware.html#ram-versus-vram-and-why-unified-memory-helps", "p": "Hardware", "x": "RAM (system memory) VRAM (video memory) Lives on The motherboard, in slots next to the CPU The graphics card, soldered beside the GPU Used by The CPU (and the GPU, slowly, over PCIe) The GPU directly Type DDR5 GDDR7 on gaming cards, HBM on data-center cards Bandwidth About 60 to 100 GB/s About 1 TB/s on an RTX 5080, about 8 TB/s on a B200 Size Cheap to expand: 32 to 256 GB Expensive and fixed: 16 "}, {"t": "Why bandwidth sets the speed", "u": "hardware.html#why-bandwidth-sets-the-speed", "p": "Hardware", "x": "When an LLM writes text it generates one token at a time, and for every token the chip must read the model's active weights out of memory. So the generation speed is limited mainly by memory bandwidth , not raw compute: tokens per second \u2248 memory bandwidth (GB/s) \u00f7 GB of weights read per token A dense 70B model at 4-bit reads about 40 GB per token. At 273 GB/s that is at most about 7 tokens per se"}, {"t": "The hardware at a glance", "u": "hardware.html#the-hardware-at-a-glance", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Hardware Fast memory Bandwidth Class NVIDIA B200 about 180 to 192 GB HBM3e about 8 TB/s Data-center GPU (sold in 8-GPU servers) NVIDIA DGX Station (GB300) about 252 GB HBM3e, plus about 496 GB LPDDR5X (about 748 GB total) about 7 TB/s on the HBM, about 400 GB/s on the CPU memory Desktop data-center system Apple M5 Ultra (Mac Studio) up to 512 GB unified about 1.2 TB/s Desktop Apple M5 Max up to 12"}, {"t": "Speed ceilings: how fast each could generate", "u": "hardware.html#speed-ceilings-how-fast-each-could-generate", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Ceiling = bandwidth \u00f7 GB read per token. \"Does not fit\" means the weights cannot sit in that machine's fast memory. Real speeds are usually 40% to 70% of these numbers. Hardware Dense 70B, 4-bit (about 40 GB per token) MoE 125B, about 6B active, 4-bit (about 3.5 GB per token) gpt-oss-20b (about 2 GB per token) B200 about 200 tokens/s about 2,000 tokens/s about 4,000 tokens/s DGX Station about 180 "}, {"t": "The best each can do", "u": "hardware.html#the-best-each-can-do", "p": "Hardware", "x": "NVIDIA B200 Speed: about 8 TB/s per GPU, the fastest here by a wide margin, and eight of them in a server are linked by NVLink. Best at: training large models, and serving the biggest models to thousands of users at once with high throughput. It is also the best single chip for prompt processing. Limits: not a desktop product, very expensive, usually rented by the hour from a cloud. See the enterp"}, {"t": "Linux x86 versus ARM", "u": "hardware.html#linux-x86-versus-arm", "p": "Hardware", "x": "The machines above use two different CPU families. x86 (Intel and AMD) is the long-standing standard for PCs and servers. ARM is a more power-efficient design used in phones, Apple's M-series chips, the NVIDIA Grace CPU in the DGX Station and the GB10 chip in the DGX Spark. Linux runs on both. x86 (Intel, AMD) ARM (Apple M-series, NVIDIA Grace, Graviton) Typical AI machines Gaming PCs and workstat"}, {"t": "What each can run (rules of thumb)", "u": "hardware.html#what-each-can-run-rules-of-thumb", "p": "Hardware", "x": "DGX Spark (128 GB): models up to about 100 to 120B parameters at 4-bit, best when they are MoE so generation stays fast. Mac Studio (512 GB): 4-bit models up to roughly 400B+ parameters, or a mid-size model at high precision with a very long context. DGX Station: models in the several-hundred-billion to trillion-parameter range when quantized, with the speed to serve several people at once."}, {"t": "Combining machines, and how they compare", "u": "hardware.html#combining-machines-and-how-they-compare", "p": "Hardware", "x": "You can link machines to run a model that does not fit on one. The pieces are connected over a network and the model is split across them (layers on one machine and layers on the next, or experts spread out). The link speed decides how well it works. Setup How it is combined Result Two DGX Sparks Direct high-speed link (ConnectX-7 networking) as an officially supported pair 256 GB combined, for mo"}, {"t": "Which one for what", "u": "hardware.html#which-one-for-what", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Goal Pick Run the largest open models on one desk, quietly Mac Studio M5 Ultra 512 GB Fastest generation for models under 16 GB RTX 5080 (or other 16 GB+ NVIDIA card) CUDA development and fine-tuning with 128 GB DGX Spark Serve a team with huge models at full speed DGX Station Train or serve at cloud scale B200 servers (rented) Portable, mid-size models Laptop with M5 Max 128 GB Small to mid-size "}, {"t": "Sources", "u": "hardware.html#sources", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Figures come from vendor pages and secondary coverage found in November 2026. Verify before buying. Apple debuts M5 Pro and M5 Max (MacRumors) Mac Studio with M5 Max and M5 Ultra (9to5Mac) Strata benchmark: RTX 5080 16 GB, 150 tok/s decode (GitHub issue 3620) Strata benchmark: RTX 5080 16 GB on an older PC, about 98 tok/s (GitHub issue 3859) M6 MacBook Pro memory bandwidth split (MacRumors) Apple'"}, {"t": "PCIe: the road between the parts", "u": "hardware.html#pcie-the-road-between-the-parts", "p": "Hardware", "x": "PCIe is the connection that links the GPU, SSDs and other devices to the CPU. A PCIe 5.0 x16 slot (the usual graphics-card slot) moves about 64 GB/s each way, far less than the GPU's own memory. That is why a model should live entirely in the GPU's memory: every time the GPU has to fetch weights acr"}, {"t": "The memory hierarchy", "u": "hardware.html#the-memory-hierarchy", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Level Example Approx. bandwidth Size GPU memory (HBM or GDDR) B200, RTX 5080 1 to 8 TB/s 16 to 290 GB Unified memory Apple M-series, DGX Spark 150 GB/s to 1.2 TB/s 16 to 512 GB System RAM (DDR5) Desktop PC 60 to 100 GB/s 32 to 256 GB PCIe link to the GPU PCIe 5.0 x16 about 64 GB/s - NVMe SSD PCIe 5."}, {"t": "How much slower it gets", "u": "hardware.html#how-much-slower-it-gets", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Example: a 24 GB model on a 16 GB RTX 5080, with 16 GB of it in VRAM (about 960 GB/s) and the remaining 8 GB in DDR5 RAM (about 80 GB/s). Per token the machine reads each part once: Case Time per token Ceiling speed All 24 GB in VRAM (a card with enough memory) 24 \u00f7 960 = 0.025 s about 40 tokens/s 1"}, {"t": "Unified memory: one pool", "u": "hardware.html#unified-memory-one-pool", "p": "Hardware", "x": "Apple's M-series chips and the NVIDIA DGX Spark put the CPU and GPU on the same chip package sharing one pool of memory . There is no VRAM versus RAM split and no PCIe copy: the GPU can use almost all of the memory at full speed. (NVIDIA's Grace-based DGX Station is a related design: the CPU and GPU"}, {"t": "Advantages in plain terms", "u": "hardware.html#advantages-in-plain-terms", "p": "Hardware", "x": "x86 wins on: compatibility (everything just installs), raw GPU speed with a discrete NVIDIA card, flexibility (upgrade and mix parts), and the largest ecosystem for training and servers. ARM wins on: efficiency (performance per watt), quiet and portable machines, and unified memory that lets a small"}, {"t": "Creating an LLM", "u": "llm.html", "p": "Build a language model from scratch on one GPU", "x": "Creating an LLM Building a language model from scratch: what fits on one RTX 5070 Ti, the seven stages, a starter configuration, and when to rent GPUs. Written Nov 4, 2026 by Adam. Not from the class This page is a practical guide added on top of the course content. It puts the ideas from Deep Learn"}, {"t": "Summary", "u": "llm.html#summary", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "On one RTX 5070 Ti you can train a GPT-2-class model (about 125M parameters) from random weights in roughly a day, and it will write fluent but shallow English. That is the right first project: you learn every stage, and the same code scales to larger models on rented GPUs. \"From scratch\" here means you build the tokenizer, collect the training text, define the transformer, and pretrain it startin"}, {"t": "What fits on your hardware", "u": "llm.html#what-fits-on-your-hardware", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "A 125M model is the sweet spot for an RTX 5070 Ti: about 15 hours of training for a compute-optimal run. Beyond about 350M parameters, training runs take weeks locally and memory gets tight. Model size Training tokens Est. time on 5070 Ti Training memory What you get ~10M ~200M (TinyStories-scale) ~5\u201310 min < 2 GB Coherent children's stories; proves the code works ~125M (GPT-2 small) 2.5B (compute"}, {"t": "The build at a glance", "u": "llm.html#the-build-at-a-glance", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "Pretraining (step 4) uses almost all of the compute; every other stage takes minutes to an hour. If evaluation disappoints, the fix is usually more tokens or a bigger model, so you go back to pretraining. tokenizer -> data shards -> model -> PRETRAIN (hours to days) -> chat SFT -> evaluate -> export to GGUF ^ | +--- more tokens or a bigger model ----+"}, {"t": "Step-by-step", "u": "llm.html#step-by-step", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "Seven stages. Get each one working on a tiny model before scaling up. Tokenizer. Train a byte-level BPE tokenizer (32k\u201350k vocabulary) on a sample of your training text with Hugging Face tokenizers , or start by reusing the GPT-2 tokenizer through tiktoken . Reserve special tokens now for chat later ( <|user|> , <|assistant|> , <|end|> ). See minbpe on the AI Pioneers page. Training data. For a fi"}, {"t": "Starter configuration (~125M parameters)", "u": "llm.html#starter-configuration-125m-parameters", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "This is GPT-2 small's shape with modern components, sized for one 16 GB card. For the 10M test run, use 6 layers, 6 heads, width 384 and context 256 on TinyStories. That is about 10M non-embedding parameters; use a small 4k to 8k vocabulary, because a 50k GPT-2 vocabulary would add about 19M embedding parameters. Setting Value Layers / attention heads / width 12 / 12 / 768 Context length 1,024 tok"}, {"t": "Scaling up: when to rent GPUs", "u": "llm.html#scaling-up-when-to-rent-gpus", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "Rent an 8\u00d7H100 node once you want a model that can hold a basic conversation. A few hundred dollars there buys more than a month of local training. Karpathy's nanochat runs the full pipeline on such a node with a single script (see the AI Pioneers page). nanochat run Parameters Time on 8\u00d7H100 Approx. cost Result d20 speedrun ~561M ~4 hours ~$100 Basic chatbot, \"like talking to a kindergartener\" GP"}, {"t": "Learning path and reference code", "u": "llm.html#learning-path-and-reference-code", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "Write the code yourself once, then switch to a tested codebase for real runs. \u2610 Week 1: Karpathy's Neural Networks: Zero to Hero series, through \"Let's build GPT\" and \"Let's build the GPT tokenizer.\" \u2610 Week 2: Write your own ~10M model and train it on TinyStories. Success = coherent stories after about 10 minutes. \u2610 Week 3: Follow \"Let's reproduce GPT-2 (124M)\" from the same series and run the 125"}, {"t": "Sources", "u": "llm.html#sources", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "RTX 5070 Ti specifications (Flopper) karpathy/nanochat (GitHub) nanochat GPT-2 time and cost (Towards AI) nanochat d32 run details (repo fork README) FineWeb-Edu dataset \u00b7 TinyStories dataset \u00b7 SmolTalk dataset Hoffmann et al., Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models (Chinchilla, 2022)"}, {"t": "How the estimates were made", "u": "llm.html#how-the-estimates-were-made", "p": "Creating an LLM", "x": "Compute: training compute is about 6 \u00d7 parameters \u00d7 tokens. The card peaks at about 88 TFLOPS BF16 dense. Real training reaches roughly 40% of peak, so about 35 TFLOPS. Compute-optimal: uses the Chinchilla rule of about 20 tokens per parameter. Memory rule of thumb: full training with AdamW in mixed"}, {"t": "Finetuning an LLM", "u": "fine-tuning.html", "p": "A QLoRA pipeline for improving a local LLM on one 16 GB GPU", "x": "Finetuning an LLM The recommended pipeline for improving a local language model on one 16 GB GPU. Written Nov 4, 2026 by Adam. Not from the class This page is a practical guide added on top of the course content. It builds on Deep Learning (transfer learning) and Generative AI (fine-tuning versus RA"}, {"t": "Summary", "u": "fine-tuning.html#summary", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "The recommended way to improve a local model on one 16 GB GPU is: build an eval set, ground the model with retrieval, optimize the prompt, then distill the best verified outputs into a QLoRA adapter and ship it as a quantized GGUF. Everything past that is optional and only triggered by a measured failure. This version corrects an earlier draft playbook in four places: Model IDs and memory math. Th"}, {"t": "Hardware and model choices", "u": "fine-tuning.html#hardware-and-model-choices", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "Target machine: RTX 5070 Ti (16 GB VRAM) with 64 GB system RAM. Qwen3.5-9B is the safer default for training on this card. Gemma 4 12B fits for inference but is tight for QLoRA at long context. Model Hugging Face ID Released Weights at 16-bit Notes Qwen3.5-9B Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B Mar 2, 2026 ~18 GB Ships with a vision encoder. Load with Unsloth (the text-only loader is enough for text fine-tuning) and "}, {"t": "The pipeline", "u": "fine-tuning.html#the-pipeline", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "Six core steps, in order. The benchmark from step 1 is the single yardstick for every later decision. Steps 1\u20134 build the data; steps 5\u20136 build the model. A model that doesn't beat the prompted base goes to the optional fixes, then back through training. Build the evaluation set first. 50\u2013100 real domain questions with known-good answers and a scoring script (schema valid, citations correct, answe"}, {"t": "Recommended QLoRA configuration (16 GB)", "u": "fine-tuning.html#recommended-qlora-configuration-16-gb", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "Start from these values and change one at a time, judged by the benchmark. Setting Value Why Base model load 4-bit NF4 Fits 9B/12B weights in ~6\u20137 GB Max sequence length 4,096 (raise to 8,192 only if examples need it) Retrieved context plus reasoning; memory grows with length LoRA rank / alpha r = 16, alpha = 16\u201332 (r = 32 for harder reasoning) Standard starting point Target modules q, k, v, o, ga"}, {"t": "Optional and experimental steps", "u": "fine-tuning.html#optional-and-experimental-steps", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "Use these only when the benchmark shows a specific problem the core pipeline didn't fix. Technique Use it when Status Caveats GRPO (reinforcement learning after SFT) The task has a reward a script can check (math, schema, citation verifier) Established; supported by Unsloth on low VRAM Needs a reliable reward; a loose reward gets gamed Directional ablation (\"abliteration\") A consistent, single beh"}, {"t": "Risks and pitfalls", "u": "fine-tuning.html#risks-and-pitfalls", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "Self-distillation has a ceiling. Training on the same model's best prompted outputs mostly buys shorter prompts and faster inference, not new capability. For real gains, generate the dataset with a stronger teacher model. Easy-case bias. Filtering for only the top-scoring outputs drops hard examples, so the model gets worse on exactly the cases that matter. Benchmark leakage. If test questions or "}, {"t": "Day-1 checklist", "u": "fine-tuning.html#day-1-checklist", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "\u2610 Write a 50-question benchmark with a scoring script and a held-out test split \u2610 Load clean domain knowledge into wiki/ and turn on trace logging \u2610 Optimize the prompt (DSPy / GEPA) and record the prompted-base score \u2610 Harvest 300\u2013500 verified, context-grounded examples into dataset.jsonl \u2610 Train QLoRA on Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B with the baseline config (r = 16, lr 2e-4, batch 1 \u00d7 8, 4k context) \u2610 Merge,"}, {"t": "Sources", "u": "fine-tuning.html#sources", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B model card (Hugging Face) Gemma 4 model overview and memory requirements (Google AI for Developers) Gemma 4 12B release guide (Digital Applied, Jun 2026) karpathy/autoresearch (GitHub) Meta-Harness: End-to-End Optimization of Model Harnesses (arXiv 2603.28052)"}, {"t": "What fits on 16 GB", "u": "fine-tuning.html#what-fits-on-16-gb", "p": "Finetuning an LLM", "x": "Training (QLoRA, 4-bit base): 9B fits comfortably at 4k\u20138k context with batch 1. 12B fits at batch 1 with shorter context; expect to drop to 4k. Merging and weight edits (16-bit): neither model fits in VRAM. Use CPU offload into the 64 GB of system RAM. Inference: 9B at Q8_0 (~9.5 GB) or 12B at Q4_K"}, {"t": "Loops", "u": "loops.html", "p": "Repeatable loops for agents, engineering, research and writing", "x": "Loops 44 repeatable loops for agents, engineering, management, product, data science, research and writing. Each one lists where it comes from, what each stage or worker does, and when it stops. To design a new loop, start here and borrow the stage you are missing. Not from the class This page is a "}, {"t": "AI agents", "u": "loops.html#ai-agents", "p": "Loops", "x": "ReAct Reason \u2192 Act \u2192 Observe Origin: AI research (Yao et al., 2022). The basic loop behind most tool-using agents. Reason: The agent thinks out loud about what it knows and what to do next. Act: It calls one tool, such as search, a file read or a shell command. Observe: It reads the tool's result and adds it to its context, then reasons again. Exit: The agent has enough to answer, or it hits a ste"}, {"t": "Software engineering", "u": "loops.html#software-engineering", "p": "Loops", "x": "TDD Red \u2192 Green \u2192 Refactor Origin: Kent Beck, Extreme Programming. For feature code and bug fixes. Red: Write one small test for the next behavior and watch it fail. Green: Write the least code that makes the test pass. Refactor: Clean up names and duplication while the tests stay green. Exit: Every required behavior has a passing test. Incident response Detect \u2192 Triage \u2192 Mitigate \u2192 Resolve \u2192 Post"}, {"t": "Management and quality", "u": "loops.html#management-and-quality", "p": "Loops", "x": "PDCA Plan \u2192 Do \u2192 Check \u2192 Act Origin: W. Edwards Deming. For any process improvement. Plan: State the goal and how you'll measure it. Do: Run the change on a small scale. Check: Compare the results with the goal. Act: If it worked, make it the standard. If not, adjust and plan again. Exit: The goal is met and the change is the new standard. OODA Observe \u2192 Orient \u2192 Decide \u2192 Act Origin: John Boyd, US"}, {"t": "Product and design", "u": "loops.html#product-and-design", "p": "Loops", "x": "Build \u2192 Measure \u2192 Learn Origin: Eric Ries, The Lean Startup. For product bets and MVPs. Build: Ship the smallest version that tests one assumption. Measure: Track a metric tied to that assumption, such as signups, retention or conversion. Learn: Decide whether to persevere, pivot or kill the idea. Exit: The assumption is proven or disproven with data. Design Thinking Empathize \u2192 Define \u2192 Ideate \u2192 "}, {"t": "Data and ML", "u": "loops.html#data-and-ml", "p": "Loops", "x": "CRISP-DM Business understanding \u2192 Data understanding \u2192 Data preparation \u2192 Modeling \u2192 Evaluation \u2192 Deployment Origin: A cross-industry data mining standard from 1999. For data and ML projects. Business understanding: Define the question and what success looks like. Data understanding: Explore the data's sources, quality and gaps. Data preparation: Clean, join and engineer features. Modeling: Train "}, {"t": "Research", "u": "loops.html#research", "p": "Loops", "x": "These three loops are built from the loops above. Run them in order: Deep Research gathers the knowledge, Hypothesis tests it and Tool Scout turns it into a decision. Kolb is for learning a subject yourself. Deep Research Frame \u2192 Decompose \u2192 Search \u2192 Synthesize \u2192 Critique \u2192 Fill gaps \u2192 Record Built from: Orchestrator \u2192 Workers, ReAct, Evaluator-Optimizer, Reflexion. For questions answered from the"}, {"t": "Writing", "u": "loops.html#writing", "p": "Loops", "x": "Writing Process, Editor loop, Working Backwards and Critical Response Process are established methods. AI Slop Grader and Persuasion Grader are built from Evaluator-Optimizer and Debate. Run the Editor loop first, then the graders on the near-final draft. Writing Process Plan \u2192 Draft \u2192 Review (recursive) Origin: Linda Flower and John Hayes's cognitive process model of writing (1981), the basis of "}, {"t": "Template for a new loop", "u": "loops.html#template-for-a-new-loop", "p": "Loops", "x": "Answer all nine questions before running a new loop. A blank answer usually means the loop will stall or never stop. Question What to decide Example (Team-solve) Goal What does \"done\" mean, in one sentence? All acceptance tests pass. Stages What runs, in what order? Plan \u2192 2 Ideators \u2192 Solver \u2192 Manager \u2192 Repair \u2192 Test. Roles Who or which agent owns each stage? The manager plans and grades; the ide"}, {"t": "Rules for every loop", "u": "loops.html#rules-for-every-loop", "p": "Loops", "x": "Define \"done\" before starting. If you cannot test it, you cannot loop on it. Grade with a verifier, not a vibe. Use tests, linters, metrics or a written rubric. Separate the builder from the reviewer. A reviewer with fresh context catches what the author cannot. Cap the number of iterations. Set a round limit and an escalation path for when it is hit. Write lessons to a file. Reflections, root cau"}, {"t": "Sources", "u": "loops.html#sources", "p": "Loops", "x": "karpathy/autoresearch The New Stack: Karpathy's 630-line AutoResearch script Karpathy Loop Engineering (mythos.one; a vendor write-up. Other coverage says \"loop engineering\" is a community label that Karpathy did not coin himself) Karpathy: A Recipe for Training Neural Networks (2019) Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville: Deep Learning, Practical Methodology Wikipedia: Ian Goodfellow Wikipedia: Alec R"}, {"t": "History of AI", "u": "history.html", "p": "Eight eras from 1943 to today: symbolists vs connectionists, winters, deep learning, transformers", "x": "History of AI Eighty years of AI in eight eras. The story is a tug-of-war between two rival ideas about what intelligence is, and the way each side won, lost and came back explains why AI looks the way it does today. Not from the class This page is a reference added on top of the course content. Dat"}, {"t": "The two camps", "u": "history.html#the-two-camps", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Symbolists Connectionists Core belief Intelligence is formal logic and hand-coded rules Intelligence emerges from statistical patterns across artificial neurons Approach Write down the knowledge: rules, search, logic Learn the knowledge from data by adjusting weights Also called GOFAI (Good Old-Fashioned AI) Neural networks, deep learning Led by Minsky, McCarthy, Newell, Simon Rosenblatt, later Hi"}, {"t": "Timeline at a glance", "u": "history.html#timeline-at-a-glance", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Years Era Defining moment 1943\u20131956 Foundations First artificial neuron, Turing Test, Dartmouth names the field 1957\u20131969 The early split Symbolic programs vs the Perceptron 1969\u20131979 First AI winter The XOR critique and the Lighthill Report 1980\u20131993 Expert systems and backpropagation Rule-based boom, then the second winter 1993\u20132011 The statistical turn Deep Blue, SVMs, random forests, LSTM 2012"}, {"t": "1. Cybernetic and mathematical foundations (1943\u20131956)", "u": "history.html#1-cybernetic-and-mathematical-foundations-1943-1956", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Before AI had a name, mathematicians and neuroscientists were asking whether human thought was a form of computation. The first artificial neuron (1943). Neurophysiologist Warren McCulloch and logician Walter Pitts showed how simple on/off artificial neurons could be wired together to carry out logical propositions (AND, OR, NOT). Turing's framework (1950). In Computing Machinery and Intelligence "}, {"t": "2. The early split: symbols vs the Perceptron (1957\u20131969)", "u": "history.html#2-the-early-split-symbols-vs-the-perceptron-1957-1969", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Right after Dartmouth the field divided into two camps that would battle for decades. The symbolic camp. Led by Minsky, McCarthy (who invented the Lisp language in 1958), Newell and Simon. Their programs included the General Problem Solver (1957) , MIT's chatbot ELIZA (1966) , which imitated a psychotherapist with simple pattern-matching and string replacement, and SRI's robot Shakey (1966\u20131972) ,"}, {"t": "3. The XOR crisis and the first AI winter (1969\u20131979)", "u": "history.html#3-the-xor-crisis-and-the-first-ai-winter-1969-1979", "p": "History of AI", "x": "By the late 1960s the hype met a mathematical and computational wall. Neural nets set back (1969). Minsky and Seymour Papert's book Perceptrons proved that a single-layer perceptron cannot solve problems that are not linearly separable, the classic example being the XOR (exclusive OR) gate. Nobody had yet popularized a way to train multi-layer networks (backpropagation was described earlier but no"}, {"t": "4. Expert systems, backpropagation and the second winter (1980\u20131993)", "u": "history.html#4-expert-systems-backpropagation-and-the-second-winter-1980-1993", "p": "History of AI", "x": "In the 1980s AI pivoted from general intelligence to narrow, commercial knowledge, while a small group quietly solved the neural-network training problem. Expert systems (1980\u20131987). Instead of learning, systems such as MYCIN (medical diagnosis) and Carnegie Mellon's XCON (which reportedly saved Digital Equipment Corporation about $40M a year configuring VAX orders) used thousands of hand-written "}, {"t": "5. The statistical turn and classical machine learning (1993\u20132011)", "u": "history.html#5-the-statistical-turn-and-classical-machine-learning-1993-2011", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Wary of the \"AI\" label, researchers rebranded their work as machine learning , informatics or pattern recognition, and replaced hand-written rules with probability and statistics. Brute-force search and classical ML. In 1997 IBM's Deep Blue beat world chess champion Garry Kasparov, evaluating around 200 million positions per second with alpha-beta pruning and custom chips. In industry, support vec"}, {"t": "6. Deep learning ignites: GPUs, ImageNet and RL (2012\u20132016)", "u": "history.html#6-deep-learning-ignites-gpus-imagenet-and-rl-2012-2016", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Neural networks already had the right math (backpropagation and CNNs) but lacked two ingredients: large labeled datasets and fast parallel matrix computation . The AlexNet moment (2012). Fei-Fei Li's team built ImageNet (about 14 million labeled images). In 2012 Geoffrey Hinton and his students Alex Krizhevsky and Ilya Sutskever trained a deep CNN, AlexNet , on two consumer NVIDIA GTX 580 GPUs usi"}, {"t": "7. The Transformer and the scaling era (2017\u20132022)", "u": "history.html#7-the-transformer-and-the-scaling-era-2017-2022", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Before 2017, language models used RNNs and LSTMs, which read text one word at a time and were hard to parallelize across thousands of GPUs. \"Attention Is All You Need\" (June 2017). Eight Google researchers (Vaswani et al.) dropped recurrence and introduced the Transformer . With self-attention , the model processes all tokens in a passage in parallel and relates words however far apart they are. S"}, {"t": "8. Alignment, reasoning models and agents (2022\u2013present)", "u": "history.html#8-alignment-reasoning-models-and-agents-2022-present", "p": "History of AI", "x": "A raw pre-trained model just continues documents. Ask it a question and it may reply with five more questions. RLHF and the ChatGPT moment (2022). Supervised fine-tuning plus reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF, using methods such as PPO; the InstructGPT work by Ouyang and colleagues built on earlier work by Christiano, Ziegler and others) turned raw predictors into helpful assistants"}, {"t": "Patterns worth remembering", "u": "history.html#patterns-worth-remembering", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Hype, wall, winter. Each wave overpromised, hit a limit (XOR, combinatorial explosion, brittle rules) and lost funding, then returned with a new ingredient. Three ingredients repeat. Better algorithms, more data and more compute. Deep learning needed all three at once. Learning beat hand-coding. Rules written by experts lost to systems that learn from data, as the symbolists' expert systems lost t"}, {"t": "Sources", "u": "history.html#sources", "p": "History of AI", "x": "Standard accounts: Turing (1950), the Dartmouth proposal (1955), Minsky and Papert, Perceptrons (1969), Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams (1986), Krizhevsky et al. (2012), Vaswani et al., Attention Is All You Need (2017) , and Chinchilla (2022) ."}, {"t": "AI Pioneers", "u": "pioneers.html", "p": "Goodfellow, Karpathy and Radford, plus a tour of Karpathy's GitHub", "x": "AI Pioneers Three researchers whose work shaped modern generative AI: Ian Goodfellow, Andrej Karpathy and Alec Radford. Each section covers what they researched and built, with a timeline. Karpathy also gets a full tour of his GitHub projects. Not from the class This page was added on top of the cou"}, {"t": "Ian Goodfellow", "u": "pioneers.html#ian-goodfellow", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "Goodfellow is best known for inventing generative adversarial networks (GANs) . The idea: two networks compete. A generator makes fake samples, a discriminator tries to tell fake from real, and each pushes the other to improve until the fakes look real. It is the \"GAN\" row in the Generative AI table, and it launched a decade of image-synthesis research. What they researched and created GANs (2014)"}, {"t": "Andrej Karpathy", "u": "pioneers.html#andrej-karpathy", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "Karpathy is a researcher, engineer and teacher known for making deep learning understandable. He helped found OpenAI, ran computer vision for Tesla Autopilot, and then built a large following by teaching how language models work from scratch. What they researched and created Image captioning (PhD, Stanford, advisor Fei-Fei Li). Work combining CNNs and RNNs so a model could describe images and regi"}, {"t": "Karpathy's GitHub projects, explained", "u": "pioneers.html#karpathy-s-github-projects-explained", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "Profile: github.com/karpathy . This section assumes you have never opened GitHub. If you have, skip to the project map . Five words you need first GitHub is a website where programmers publish their code. A repo (repository) is one project's folder on it. A gist is a single shared file or note. Stars are like bookmarks, so a high number means many people found the project useful. An LLM (large lan"}, {"t": "Alec Radford", "u": "pioneers.html#alec-radford", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "Radford is a researcher who works quietly and publishes few papers, but he was first author on several that defined modern generative AI. Much of the field's move from \"train a model for one task\" to \"pretrain a big model, then adapt it\" runs through his work. What they researched and created DCGAN (2015). With Luke Metz and Soumith Chintala, a set of design rules that made convolutional GANs stab"}, {"t": "The common thread", "u": "pioneers.html#the-common-thread", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "All three worked on the same arc. Goodfellow's GANs showed that networks could generate convincing data. Radford's DCGAN, GPT, CLIP and Whisper work showed that large generatively pretrained models transfer to many tasks. Karpathy then taught the field how those systems are built, with code short enough to read. If you want to follow the arc yourself, read Deep Learning , then Generative AI , then"}, {"t": "What they researched and created", "u": "pioneers.html#what-they-researched-and-created", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "GANs (2014). Introduced in a paper with colleagues at the Universit\u00e9 de Montr\u00e9al, including their PhD advisors Yoshua Bengio and Aaron Courville. Adversarial examples. Co-authored early work showing that tiny, deliberate changes to an input can fool a neural network, and proposed training on such ex"}, {"t": "Timeline", "u": "pioneers.html#timeline", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "When Event Early Studied at Stanford (BS and MS, with Andrew Ng) 2014 Publishes the GAN paper and the adversarial-examples work Feb 2015 PhD in machine learning, Universit\u00e9 de Montr\u00e9al 2015 Joins Google Brain Mar 2016 Moves to the newly founded OpenAI; the Deep Learning textbook appears the same yea"}, {"t": "What they researched and created", "u": "pioneers.html#what-they-researched-and-created-2", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "Image captioning (PhD, Stanford, advisor Fei-Fei Li). Work combining CNNs and RNNs so a model could describe images and regions in sentences. That is the \"hybrid model\" idea on the Deep Learning page. The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Recurrent Neural Networks (2015). A blog post and the char-rnn co"}, {"t": "Timeline", "u": "pioneers.html#timeline-2", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "When Event 1986 Born in Bratislava; moves to Toronto at 15 2009 / 2011 Bachelor's at the University of Toronto; master's at the University of British Columbia 2015 PhD at Stanford; starts teaching CS231n; char-rnn and the RNN blog post; helps start OpenAI Jun 2017 Becomes Tesla's director of AI Jul "}, {"t": "The common idea", "u": "pioneers.html#the-common-idea", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "Most AI code is huge and hard to read. Karpathy's habit is the opposite: shrink a big idea until it fits on one screen , so a person can read every line and understand what the technology really does. Think of it as taking a car engine apart on a table and labelling each part, instead of describing "}, {"t": "Group A: learn how AI works", "u": "pioneers.html#group-a-learn-how-ai-works", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": ""}, {"t": "Group B: build and run a real model", "u": "pioneers.html#group-b-build-and-run-a-real-model", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": ""}, {"t": "Group C: use AI as a tool", "u": "pioneers.html#group-c-use-ai-as-a-tool", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": ""}, {"t": "What they researched and created", "u": "pioneers.html#what-they-researched-and-created-3", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "DCGAN (2015). With Luke Metz and Soumith Chintala, a set of design rules that made convolutional GANs stable to train. It became the template for image GANs, and it also showed that the latent space holds meaningful directions (vector arithmetic on faces). The sentiment neuron (2017). A character-le"}, {"t": "Timeline", "u": "pioneers.html#timeline-3", "p": "AI Pioneers", "x": "When Event 2015 DCGAN paper 2017 Unsupervised sentiment neuron 2018 GPT-1 2019 GPT-2 2020 GPT-3 (co-author) 2021 CLIP and DALL-E 2022 Whisper Dec 2024 Reported to leave OpenAI for independent research 2025 Reported to advise Thinking Machines Lab"}, {"t": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html", "p": "Searchable definitions", "x": "Glossary Plain-English definitions. Type in the box to filter. Activation function A function applied to a neuron's weighted sum that lets a network model non-linear patterns. Common ones: ReLU, sigmoid, tanh, softmax. Agentic AI An AI system that plans and carries out multi-step tasks using tools, "}];